  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 328 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nAs noted above, widespread rain chances will continue thru Friday \nmorning. Southwest flow aloft will continue to bring sufficient sub-\ntropical moisture northeastward. A surface low will deepen across \nnortheast Colorado and start to move into southwest Nebraska through \nthe afternoon/evening Thursday. A weak frontal boundary will extend \non the east side of this low. There still is some disagreement \nbetween guidance on how far this boundary settle beyond the KS/NE \nborder through the evening Thursday and into Friday. This will be a \nmajor factor in which areas see the highest rainfall amounts. As it \nstands now, areas east of a line from Ogallala to O'Neill could see \namounts upwards of 1.0 to 1.5\". LREF probabilities gives at 40 to 60 \npercent of this area seeing upwards of an inch where as further into \nsouthwest Nebraska still holds probabilities of 20 to 40 percent for \nrainfall amounts reaching 1.5\". This could come in the form of \nlocally heavy rain which will bring some flooding concerns. With \nlapse rates expected to remain weak along with a only modest shear, \nwhat thunderstorms do form, are not likely to become severe, but \nthis will be continue to be monitored for any changes. \n\nWidespread precipitation chances should greatly decrease in time for \nthe weekend. As the upper trough moves past Friday, it will bring in \nthe attempt for a ridge to build. This will bring in warmer, dryer \nair from the desert southwest. Daytime highs will increase back to \nnear normal to even slightly above normal across southwest and the \neastern Panhandle, with highs in the upper 70s to low 80s.",
